journalctl is a Linux command-line utility used to view, query, and manage system logs generated by systemd-journald, the centralized logging service for the systemd init system.

Unlike traditional logging mechanisms (like syslog) that save logs in plaintext files scattered across /var/log/,

systemd-journald gathers logs from the kernel, system services, and boot processes, 
storing them in a structured, indexed binary format. Because you cannot read binary files with standard tools like cat or less, journalctl acts as the dedicated translator and interface for these logs.


Key Advantages of journalctl

• Centralization: It aggregates all logs (kernel, individual system services, background daemons) into a single, unified pipeline.
• Speed: Because logs are stored in a binary, indexed format, searching and filtering through millions of lines is incredibly fast.
• Rich Metadata: Every log entry doesn't just save text; it automatically captures structured data like the exact timestamp, Process ID (PID), User ID (UID), and the specific systemd unit that triggered it.


Cheatsheet: Common journalctl Commands

To use journalctl effectively, you will usually need administrator privileges, so prefix these commands with sudo.

1. General Viewing

• journalctl – View all logs from oldest to newest (opens in a scrollable screen).
• journalctl -r – Reverse order; shows the newest logs first.
• journalctl -n 20 – Show only the last 20 log entries.
• journalctl -f – Follow live logs in real time (similar to tail -f).


2. Filtering by Service or Component

• journalctl -u nginx.service – Show logs for a specific systemd unit/service (e.g., Nginx).
• journalctl -k – Show kernel messages only (great for hardware/driver troubleshooting).


4. Filtering by Urgency / Boot

• journalctl -p err (or -p 3) – Filter by priority level. Shows only errors and critical issues (skips information/debug messages).
• journalctl --list-boots – Displays a list of all recorded system boot cycles.
• journalctl -b -1 – Show logs specifically from the previous boot cycle (crucial if the system crashed and rebooted).

